In article <2546@inews.intel.com> sstrazdu@hopi.intel.com (Stephen Strazdus) writes:
>If let each character gain proficiency you also have to
>keep track of what his proficiency is for every type of weapon.

I thought computers were good at keeping track of things?

>Also by giving a wizard a -4 to hit if he uses a two-handed sword you
>indirectly take into account the fact that wizards are usually weaker
>than say, barbarians.

No, because I give him a -6 (or whatever the current strength-adjustment code
would generate) for the two-handed sword and -4 for, say, a short sword he
might be strong enough for. I just meant a wizard who has never held a sword
in his hands might not know how to use it - thrust, swing, how do I keep my
balance, etc. If in addition the thing is so heavy that he can barely hold it
at arm's length, he's going to have even more serious problems.

>To balance this disadvantage for weaker
>characters you could restrict the use of magic by other characters.  For
>instance, there is a good chance a barbarian could not use scrolls, let
>alone read spellbooks and memorize them.

Well, I often play barbarians and can certify that this is already the case,
because of their intelligence :-)

>Ah, you may say, but what about gain ability potions?  If my
>Intellegence goes up shouldn't I be able to use scrolls and spellbooks?
>If my Strength goes up shouldn't I be able to use a two-handes sword.
>No!  Are you really going to learn how to read in a dungeon when
>monster's are breathing down your back?

If you don't know how to read, you have a zero chance of reading a scroll and
you won't learn with a potion of gain ability, so the 'r' command could
legitimately say "Sorry, you never bothered to learn how to read". But if you
know how to use a sword and your strength made it difficult, I don't see why
this restriction shouldn't be lifted when you become stronger. I also don't
see why you shouldn't be able to learn a new weapon (is a weapon more
complicated than a car? Does it take years of practice to learn to drive
reasonably well?). A food ration amounts to a full meal, and lasts 800 turns.
This means one day is around 1500 turns, we're not talking about a couple
hours of practice but several days/weeks spent hacking at monsters (strangely
enough, you never need to sleep in the dungeon :-) ).

>Does
>someone know if you do more damage if you have a higher strength?

Yes, you do.

>As far as preists and swords are concerned, I think this should make
>your god angry.  I doubt if a god would directly stop you from doing
>something, but he certainly wouldn't help you later.

Well, this is something I never understood. Why should my god be pissed off
if I use a sword? If my god is a god of healing, I *might* understand, but I
find it ridiculous in AD&D to have a CE cleric worshipping Mars (or similar war
god) and you can't use a sword because the rules say it "upsets the boss".

